<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sirimavo Bandaranaike</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1916">1916</a>–<a href="/years/2000">2000</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sirimavo Bandaranaike was a Sri Lankan politician who in 1960 became the world's first woman prime minister, leading the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> Freedom Party after the assassination of her husband, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/s-w-r-d-bandaranaike">S. W. R. D. Bandaranaike</a>. She served three terms, 1960–1965, 1970–1977 and 1994–2000, nationalising key industries and overseeing the 1972 constitution that made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a> the republic of Sri Lanka.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sirimavo-bandaranaike.jpg" alt="Sirimavo Bandaranaike" width="320" height="382" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Indiragandhifan · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bandaranaike_1974.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sirimavo Bandaranaike was born Sirima Ratwatte on 17 April 1916 at Ratnapura in British Ceylon, into a Sinhalese Kandyan aristocratic family. Her father, Barnes Ratwatte, was a native headman and politician, and her mother, Rosalind Hilda Mahawalatenne Kumarihamy, was an informal Ayurvedic physician. She was educated in Anglican, English-medium schools but remained a Buddhist and spoke Sinhala as well as English.</p><p>On 2 October 1940 she married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/s-w-r-d-bandaranaike">S. W. R. D. Bandaranaike</a>, in a wedding the press called the wedding of the century. They had two daughters, Sunethra and Chandrika, and a son, Anura. As the wife of the founder of the Sri Lanka Freedom Party, she acted as hostess and informal adviser and worked on the welfare of rural women and girls. After his assassination in 1959 she was persuaded to lead the party.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>The Freedom Party won the July 1960 election by a landslide, and on 21 July 1960 she was sworn in as prime minister, the first woman to hold the post anywhere. As she was not a member of Parliament, she was appointed to the Senate. In her first term she nationalised banking, foreign trade, insurance, the petroleum industry and funded parochial schools, and she took control of seven newspapers. Her government survived a plot by military officers in 1962. She followed a policy of non-alignment, attended the first four summits of the Non-Aligned Movement and chaired it in 1976–1977.</p><p>The 1965 election made her Leader of the Opposition, and the United Front won a large majority in 1970. Her second government faced an insurrection by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janatha-vimukthi-peramuna">Janatha Vimukthi Peramuna</a> in 1971, and in May 1972 a new constitution replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a> with the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>. It recognised the supremacy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, made Sinhala the only official language and abolished the Senate. In 1975 she created an agency on women's issues, later the Ministry of Women and Child Affairs. She lost heavily in 1977. A 1978 constitution introduced a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/presidential-democracy">presidential system</a>, and she returned as prime minister in 1994 under her daughter Chandrika Kumaratunga, who had become president.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Bandaranaike died on 10 October 2000 of a heart attack, on the day of a parliamentary election in which she had been casting her vote. Two days of national mourning were declared.</p><p>She served more than seventeen years as prime minister across three terms. Her policies of nationalisation and of Sinhala as the official language remained points of dispute, and her language policy aggravated discontent among the Tamil population. Her children also held prominent public positions, and the Bandaranaike family became one of the leading political families of the country.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sirimavo Bandaranaike” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After his assassination in 1959, his widow <b>Sirimavo Bandaranaike</b> became the world's first woman prime minister in 1960.</blockquote><a href="/state/dominion-of-ceylon">Dominion of Ceylon</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Baḷangoḍa, in what is now <a href="/countries/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>. Died in Kadawatha, in what is now <a href="/countries/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=6.65,80.68&amp;year=1970" aria-label="Baḷangoḍa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Sirimavo Bandaranaike"><image href="/maps/globes/g09000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4212 4433h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4212 4433h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=6.65,80.68&amp;year=1970">What was here in 1970? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
